bone squints were known for being pretty  open-minded about new people and  experiences that is until they weren't  keep watching for the bone scene that  makes us uncomfortable for all the wrong  reasons  one of the best things about bones is  the even-handed accepting nature of the  jeffersonian staff when confronted with  lifestyles they've not previously  considered it makes sense that  temperance brennan would keep an open  mind when thrust into a new culture  brennan is a forensic anthropologist but  you don't get to that profession without  studying some cultural anthropology  along the way she knew a lot more about  other cultures than her colleagues and  she wasn't afraid of shoving that fact  into people's faces she was a smart  confident woman who didn't pipe down  making her one of the strongest female  protagonists to come out of television  in the 21st century fbi agent booth on  the other hand usually has a more  reactionary view of the things he's  seeing but to his credit he didn't feel  put down when temperance knew more than  him he accepted it as a fact but these  two viewpoints still frequently came in  conflict with the show eventually coming  down in bones favor most of the time but  one time the show just completely  whiffed it season 4's episode titled the  girl in the mask is wild to behold after  years of gender discourse in the episode  a japanese forensics expert dr haru  tanaka travels to dc to help booth  investigate a death dr tanaka dresses  very androgynously and the squints  obsess over what their quote real gender  is so any guesses we don't really have  enough evidence yet no not the case  a doctor  dude or dude  the scene that goes too far lands near  the end of the episode fed up with not  knowing angela hugs dr tanaka until they  have a biological reaction to being so  close to a beautiful woman angela thus  concludes that dr tanaka is a man not  only is this extremely invasive but the  team ignores the possibility that dr  tanaka could be a gender or non-binary  nobody just asks dr tanaka what their  pronouns are which would be the polite  thing to do brennan does an excellent  job of never gendering dr tanaka always  referring to them by their full name to  obviate the need for gendered pronouns  at all the misgendering of dr tanaka has  its roots in orientalism the episode  scores some points when sweet says dr  tanaka belongs to the k subculture which  is known for its genderless style and  that tanaka's gender shouldn't matter  however the rest of the team essentially  dismisses dr tanaka's gender expression  as a japanese thing but plenty of people  all over the world identify as  non-binary and what could have been an  excellent opportunity for representation  on tv just ended up being problematic  and disrespectful the girl in the mask  only came out in 2009 which isn't that  long ago yet it manages to ultimately  damage androgynous representation on  screen by insisting upon the  significance of assigning everyone a  binary gender role in an interview after  the episode came out ali maki who  portrays tanaka spoke about how that  division emphasizes a clear cultural  divide between western and eastern  sensibilities maki discusses how she was  at first hesitant to audition as well as  how the creative team lowered her voice  register in post-production she also  provides a different type of insight  into the character and how strangely the  episode actually confronts western  culture's ideas on gender maki explains  i thought it was hilarious that the  three characters wanted to know man or  woman man or woman it was so important  to them maki who is japanese american  goes on to clarify how japan is far more  accepting of androgynous identities that  don't neatly fit into binaries she goes  on to state in japan you know it's like  no one would really care you're walking  down the street and it wouldn't even be  an issue whether the episode succeeded  in getting that idea across is open to  interpretation but it's clear japan has  been ahead of the curve when it comes to  accepting individuals of all gender  identities few would argue the episode  could have done more to be more  inclusive of non-binary identities  perhaps by casting a non-binary actor in  the role while television still has a  long way to go with greater lgbtq plus  representation we're in a much better  place today than we were even 10 years  ago check out one of our newest videos  right here plus even more looper videos  about your favorite modern classic  series are coming soon subscribe to our  youtube channel and hit the bell so you  don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

